===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|cisterne}}, from {{der|en|fro|cisterne}} (Modern French {{m|en|citerne}}) from {{der|en|la|cisterna}}, from {{m|la|cista||box}}, from {{der|en|grc|κίστη||box}}. {{doublet|en|cisterna}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈsɪs.tən/|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈsɪs.tɚn/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-cistern.ogg|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-cistern.ogg|a=AU}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪstə(ɹ)n|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|Q838858}}A [[reservoir]] or [[tank]] for holding [[water]], especially for [[catching]] and [[holding]] [[rainwater]] for later use.
#* '''1913''', A.C. Cotter, ''[[w:Catholic Encyclopedia|Catholic Encyclopedia]]'', "[[s:Catholic Encyclopedia (1913)/Wells in Scripture|Wells in Scripture]]",
#*: Their extreme necessity is attested by the countless number of old, unused '''cisterns''' with which the Holy Land is literally honeycombed.
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1989|author={{w|Octavia E. Butler}}|title={{w|Lilith's Brood|Imago}}|chapter=Part III, Chapter 7|page=211|text=On a broad ledge near the top, we found a stone cabin. Higher up was a '''cistern''' and a few more terraces.}}
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=2001
|author=Philip J. King; Lawrence E. Stager
|title=Life in Biblical Israel
|chapter=3
|isbn=0664221483
|page=126
|passage='''Cisterns''' (''bôr'', ''bō'r''), mentioned frequently in the Bible, are artificial reservoirs, usually cut into bedrock, for collecting and conserving rain runoff from roofs and courtyards.}}
# In a [[flush toilet]], the container in which the water used for [[flush]]ing is held; a toilet tank.
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=2003
|author=Allan Windust
|title=Waterwise House & Garden: a Guide for Sustainable Living
|chapter=9
|isbn=0643068007
|page=36
|passage=It is possible to connect your tank to your toilet '''cistern''' and/or garden, so that even if the water is not drinkable it still can be used productively to make major water savings.}}
# A shallow, typically [[oval]], vessel made of [[porcelain]], [[pottery]], or [[glass]].
# The [[receptacle]] that holds the fuel in an [[oil lamp]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1977|author=K.M. Elizabeth Murray|title=Caught in the Web of Words|publisher=Oxford University Press|location=Oxford|page=22|text=By next winter he was spending every evening poring over the work of Théodore Agrippa d'Aubigné on the French Reformation by the light of a little oil lamp, with a tiny '''cistern''' the size of an orange and no chimney[.]}}
# {{lb|en|Biology}} A [[cisterna]].
# The [[vessel]] surrounding the [[condenser]] in a [[steam engine]].

